548	  <section class="entry" id="tek1">
549	    <span class="index-mark">03</span>
550	    <span class="tag">Built &amp; Tested</span>
551	    <h2>Third Eye Kit</h2>
552	    <p>
553	      The first version began with a simple frustration. A cane tells you where a wall is only after you
554	      are close enough to touch it. I wanted a signal that arrived sooner, one you could feel before you
555	      needed to react.
556	    </p>
557	    <p>
558	      The result was a wearable system: smart glasses, headphone, gloves, and a GPS pack, built around
559	      ultrasonic sensors and haptic feedback. It was tested with blind users at a center in Herat,
560	      Afghanistan, where the feedback shaped nearly every revision that followed. It went on to win gold
561	      at TEKNOFEST Turkey, in the Barrier-Free Living competition.
562	    </p>
563	    <div class="meta">
564	      <span>Gold Medal, TEKNOFEST 2022</span>
565	      <span>Ultrasonic + Haptic + GPS</span>
566	      <span>Prototype cost: $112</span>
567	    </div>
568	  </section>
569	
570	  <section class="entry" id="heard">
571	    <span class="index-mark">04</span>
572	    <span class="tag">Live</span>
573	    <h2>Heard</h2>
574	    <p>
575	      Not every problem needs a sensor. Some need another voice. Heard trains blind and low-vision
576	      individuals as paid listeners, people who understand the weight of a bad day without needing it
577	      explained twice. It is not therapy, and it was never meant to be. It is one person choosing to be
578	      reachable for another.
579	    </p>
580	    <p>
581	      It has carried over a thousand calls across two hundred listeners and eleven languages, including
582	      partner centers as far as Brazil.
583	    </p>
584	    <div class="meta">
585	      <span>1,000+ calls</span>
586	      <span>200 listeners</span>
587	      <span>11 languages</span>
588	    </div>
589	    <a class="link-out" href="https://heard.teww.org">heard.teww.org</a>
590	  </section>
591	
592	  <section class="entry" id="tek2">
593	    <span class="index-mark">05</span>
594	    <span class="tag spec">In Development</span>
595	    <h2>Third Eye Kit v2</h2>
596	    <p>
597	      The next version rethinks the first one's biggest assumption, that sound should carry most of the
598	      information. This design shifts that weight onto the body itself, using full body haptic feedback
599	      as the primary channel and sound as support rather than lead. The principle behind it is short
600	      enough to fit on one line: feel, do not wait.
601	    </p>
602	    <p>
603	      It is actively under development, built on a reference specification that is not yet clinically
604	      validated and is not a certified medical device. I am stating that plainly here because precision
605	      about what is real and what is in progress matters more to me than the appearance of being further
606	      along than I am.
607	    </p>
608	  </section>
609	
610	  <section class="entry" id="research">
611	    <span class="index-mark">06</span>
612	    <span class="tag">Research</span>
613	    <h2>Research &amp; Writing</h2>
614	
615	    <div class="research-item">
616	      <h3>Concluded Today, Forgotten Tomorrow</h3>
617	      <p>
618	        A paper on how research from the Global South gets excluded from the record, not through malice
619	        but through a kind of quiet path dependence. It proposes two ideas: the Forgetting Axiom, and a
620	        graded standard I call Epistemological Completion. Third Eye Kit appears in it as a case study,
621	        an example of work built and tested outside the rooms where most research gets taken seriously.
622	      </p>
623	    </div>
624	
625	    <div class="research-item">
626	      <h3>Third Eye World</h3>
627	      <p>
628	        A research document exploring what a blind-first digital ecosystem could look like, not a
629	        screen-reader adaptation of existing platforms, but something designed from a sensory-neutral
630	        foundation from the start. Audio, braille, haptic, and low-vision visual as equal projections of
631	        one underlying data model, rather than one primary experience translated for everyone else.
632	      </p>
633	    </div>
634	
635	    <div class="research-item">
636	      <h3>Third Eye Language</h3>
637	      <p>
638	        An investigation into whether blind people need or would develop an entirely new language to
639	        describe the world. The document's own conclusion is no, that path is not justified. Blind people
640	        already use visual vocabulary competently, and the more useful direction is a design philosophy,
641	        nonvisual-first or born-accessible, rather than a literal new language.
642	      </p>
643	    </div>
644	
645	    <div class="research-item">
646	      <h3>Third Eye AI</h3>
647	      <p>
648	        Early-stage scoping for a conversational, voice-first AI layer meant to eventually connect to
649	        Third Eye Kit's sensor data. Kept intentionally tight, a buildable specification rather than a
650	        sprawling research document, since this project is still in its earliest planning stage.
651	      </p>
652	    </div>
653	  </section>
654	
655	  <section class="entry" id="about">
656	    <span class="index-mark">07</span>
657	    <span class="tag">Background</span>
658	    <h2>About</h2>
659	    <p>
660	      I am currently completing a B.S. in Software Engineering at St. Cloud State University. I have
661	      written technical guides on Arduino and Python, and I work as a certified interpreter across five
662	      languages.
663	    </p>
664	
665	    <div class="research-item">
666	      <h3>Intel Corporation, Semiconductor Manufacturing Specialist</h3>
667	      <p>
668	        Since March 2025, I have worked as a manufacturing specialist contractor at Intel in Hillsboro,
669	        Oregon, certified across seven wafer processing toolsets. I run preventive maintenance,
670	        qualification runs, and parameter monitoring under strict cleanroom protocols, and document tool
671	        deviations that feed directly into engineering decisions. When I noticed an ergonomic safety
672	        hazard in chemical disposal, I designed a gravity fed tripod fixture that eliminated it entirely.
673	        The team adopted it.
674	      </p>
675	    </div>
676	
677	    <div class="research-item">
678	      <h3>Cyborg Tech, Senior Robotics &amp; Automation Engineer</h3>
679	      <p>
680	        Over twelve years at Cyborg Tech, I built robotics, embedded systems, and industrial automation
681	        across more than twenty projects, spanning smart agriculture, manufacturing maintenance, and
682	        humanoid robotics. I deployed automated irrigation across seven commercial greenhouses that cut
683	        water use by seventy eight percent, built a remote controlled tank robot from its mechanical frame
684	        through its control firmware, and built humanoid robots with sensor fusion and real time embedded
685	        control.
686	      </p>
687	    </div>
688	  </section>
689	
690	  <section class="entry" id="book">
691	    <span class="index-mark">08</span>
692	    <span class="tag">Book, In Progress</span>
693	    <h2>The Third Eye</h2>
694	    <p>
695	      This is the book behind everything else on this page. It follows what happened after I lost most of
696	      my sight, the months of relearning a room, a hallway, a world, without it, and how that relearning
697	      became the reason Third Eye Worldwide exists at all. It is currently in progress, roughly sixty
698	      thousand words in, and being shaped with a publishing mentor toward eventual submission to literary
699	      agents.
700	    </p>
701	
702	    <button class="excerpt-toggle" id="excerptToggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="excerptBlock">
703	      Read the prologue
704	    </button>
705	
706	    <blockquote class="excerpt" id="excerptBlock" hidden>
707	      <p class="prologue-label">Prologue</p>
708	      <p>
709	        I still dream I'm blind. Even awake, I sometimes reach for walls, half afraid I'm back in the room
710	        where I first woke without sight.
711	      </p>
712	      <p>
713	        The last image I remember clearly is the crowd at the Kabul airport breaking apart in smoke. Then
714	        everything went dark.
715	      </p>
716	      <p>
717	        When I woke fifteen days later, both eyes were bandaged. Every sound felt like it was trying to
718	        find me: footsteps, bowls, the faint tap of a cane. A former Kabul professor had turned his home
719	        into a shelter for people like himself, the blind. The air smelled of medicine, boiled potatoes,
720	        and quiet fear. Someone whispered a prayer until coughing interrupted.
721	      </p>
722	      <p>
723	        At first, I hated the noise. It reminded me that I wasn't the student who built robots, led clubs,
724	        or coached chess anymore. I was a body relearning existence. The sound of other people's canes hurt
725	        me. It meant they were moving, and I was not. I sat for hours, tracing cracks on the floor like maps
726	        back to myself.
727	      </p>
728	      <p>
729	        One morning, a little boy beside me asked softly, "Does the dark ever stop?"
730	      </p>
731	      <p>
732	        I didn't know what to say. But his voice made me start listening, to everything. The smell of soap
733	        meant someone had dared to wash alone. I learned the shape of the room from how the air thickened
734	        near walls. Blindness wasn't silence anymore. It became a language of clues, a way to notice what
735	        others stopped seeing.
736	      </p>
737	      <p>
738	        Months later, surgeries gave me a few fragile hours of sight in one eye each day. I spent them
739	        carefully, testing what my hands remembered. When I soldered my first working sensor, the motor
740	        buzzed, and I froze. It sounded like one of the shelter's canes speaking back. People clapped, but
741	        inside I burned. Why should boiling an egg or fixing a wire feel miraculous. That moment decided it
742	        for me. I wanted to build something that made ordinary life feel ordinary again.
743	      </p>
744	      <p>
745	        That became my first prototype: a small wearable tool translating distance into vibration so users
746	        could feel obstacles. It wasn't perfect. Sometimes it stayed silent when it shouldn't. But every
747	        failure taught me something. Each false signal became a lesson in listening. Over months of trial
748	        and error, those lessons shaped the first working model.
749	      </p>
750	      <p>
751	        When my family fled across borders, I carried each version in my backpack. In Afghanistan,
752	        blindness often meant invisibility. In Pakistan, it became shared strength, people guiding one
753	        another with quiet precision. In Turkey, systems were organized but distant. And in America, I
754	        found technology everywhere, yet empathy often missing. The same device behaved differently in
755	        each place. A signal that worked in one hallway failed in another because the walls echoed at a
756	        different pitch. I learned that invention only succeeds when it listens, to people, to spaces, to
757	        the real textures of life.
758	      </p>
759	      <p>
760	        Those lessons became Third Eye Worldwide, a small but growing collaboration among engineers,
761	        teachers, and blind students sharing open source designs and stories. Our goal isn't to build the
762	        "best" device. It's to make independence unremarkable. During one test session, a student walked
763	        into a wall and burst out laughing. That laugh, fear turning into humor, told me we were building
764	        something real.
765	      </p>
766	      <p>
767	        My sight is still limited. Bright light stings. I can't stare at screens for long. But those limits
768	        became design rules: clarity before complexity, listening before fixing. When a student gets lost
769	        in a lesson, I slow down. When a prototype fails, I pause and listen before touching a tool.
770	        Attention has replaced urgency. I still bump into chairs sometimes, and laugh before anyone else
771	        does.
772	      </p>
773	      <p>
774	        Blindness didn't make me wise. It made me attentive. I measure progress in quieter ways now, the
775	        steadiness of a student's steps, or the pause when someone realizes they can move alone.
776	      </p>
777	      <p class="closing-line">
778	        I once thought light was something you either had or lost. Now I know it can be built, shared like
779	        code, carried as sound, passed hand to hand.
780	      </p>
781	    </blockquote>
